  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Major depressive disorder, recurrent, in remission (F33.2)</content>. Remission: PHQ-9 4. With recurrent episodes since his twenties, the risk of another is high. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide or self-harm. [S]<list><item>Continue sertraline 100 mg daily for at least a year, and discuss longer maintenance.</item><item>Psychotherapy monthly.</item><item>PHQ-9 every three months.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Tourette syndrome (F95.2)</content>. Yale Global Tic Severity Scale: total tic score 22, from 30; impairment 10, from 20. He finds the tics manageable. [S]<list><item>No medication; booster behavioral sessions if tics worsen.</item><item>Tic severity scale yearly.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression measure (PHQ-9)</td><td>At baseline and at least monthly during acute treatment; every 2 to 4 weeks in continuation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>4: remission</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Sertraline adherence and adverse effects</td><td>Every visit in the acute phase; reassess at 2 weeks after starting and after each dose change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Adherent; night sweats lessening</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Interaction review (serotonergic drugs; NSAIDs and anticoagulants with an SSRI)</td><td>At the start and with any new medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>The ibuprofen and omeprazole course after the back strain is finished; no other medicines</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk review (PHQ-9 item 9 and structured questions since the last visit), safety plan, and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of suicide or self-harm</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Psychotherapy (cognitive behavioral therapy with behavioral activation)</td><td>Offer to all with moderate or severe depression</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Monthly</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Anxiety measure (GAD-7)</td><td>At baseline and with PHQ-9 reviews</td><td>Completed today</td><td>5, from 12</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Tic severity (Yale Global Tic Severity Scale, recorded in the note; no LOINC code is used)</td><td>At diagnosis and periodically to judge the need for and response to treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Total tic score 22; impairment 10</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Comprehensive Behavioral Intervention for Tics</td><td>Offer as an initial treatment option</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Eight sessions completed in the summer</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Tic-suppressing medication (alpha-2 agonist, aripiprazole, or other)</td><td>When tics cause impairment that behavioral therapy does not relieve</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Tics manageable without medicine</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Zoster, hepatitis B, and influenza vaccines</td><td>Per the adult schedule</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Zoster dose 2, hepatitis B dose 2, and influenza vaccine given today</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>PHQ-9 response (at least a 50% fall) by three months and remission (below 5) by six months. Outcome by 2026-09-28: PHQ-9 10 at eleven weeks (from 21) and 4 at six months [S]</item><item>Back at daily writing and seeing friends again by three months. Outcome by 2026-06-22: Writing most mornings and dining with friends several times a week [S]</item><item>Fewer and less disruptive tics, judged by him and by the tic severity scale, by six months. Outcome by 2026-09-28: Yale Global Tic Severity Scale total tic score 22 from 30 and impairment 10 from 20; he finds the remaining tics manageable [S]</item><item>Safety plan in place and reviewed at every visit. Outcome by 2026-09-28: Reviewed at every visit; no suicidal thoughts at any visit [S]</item></list><paragraph>Immunizations given today: Recombinant zoster vaccine, dose 2 of 2; Hepatitis B vaccine (HepB-CpG), dose 2 of 2; Influenza vaccine, 2026-2027 season [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Guideline basis: Practice guideline recommendations summary: Treatment of tics in people with Tourette syndrome and chronic tic disorders (Pringsheim T, et al. Neurology 2019;92(19):896-906) (2019); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Major Depressive Disorder (2022); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026); Adult Immunization Schedule by Medical Condition and Other Indication (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Review in three months with PHQ-9. Neurology as needed. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
